      "id": 1,
      "text": "Anniston began as Shelly's Ridge in 1840, named for early settler Joe Shelly.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/anniston_missouri",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town became Hainley's Switch in the 1880s when a railroad switch on Jake Hainley Sr.'s land opened the line.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/anniston_missouri",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "By the 1890s the village boasted a saw mill, grist mill, and stave factory that fed the surrounding grain, stock, and cotton farms.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/anniston_missouri",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Anniston was first incorporated as a village on March 3, 1897, lost that status in 1908 over tax disputes, and was reincorporated in 1913.",
